{"qid":"Q1335260","names":["Emirate of Afghanistan"],"title":"Emirate of Afghanistan","endonym":{"native":"امارت افغانستان","lang":"fa"},"capsule":"The Emirate of Afghanistan was an absolute monarchy established by Dost Mohammad Khan in 1826 after he consolidated control of Kabul and took the title Amir, replacing the Durrani Sadozai dynasty. The emirate's existence was shaped by the Great Game between Britain and Russia: two Anglo-Afghan Wars (1838–42 and 1878–80) tested its independence, but the Treaty of Gandamak (1879) ceded foreign-policy control to Britain while leaving internal sovereignty intact. Abd al-Rahman Khan (r. 1880–1901) used British subsidy and ruthless military campaigns to forge the first centralized Afghan state, defining its modern borders through agreements with Britain (1893 Durand Line) and Russia (1885–95). The emirate ended when Amanullah Khan, having recovered full sovereignty after the Third Anglo-Afghan War (1919), declared Afghanistan a kingdom in 1926.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Post-1893 Durand Line boundary is treaty-defined and reasonably precise; the western frontier with Persia and the northern frontier with Russia were delimited by Anglo-Russian agreements; internal administrative boundaries and pre-1880 outer limits are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Afghanistan — Independence and Reform","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Afghanistan/Independence-and-reform"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Emirate of Afghanistan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirate_of_Afghanistan"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Emirate of Af